2026-05-24 01:34:52,978 - INFO - thoughts: ["Here are three possible next steps to engineer more meaningful features using the provided dataset for classifying the vehicle silhouette into distinct classes (OPEL, SAAB, BUS, VAN). Each step utilizes domain knowledge and available operations to enhance prediction power.\n\n### Step 1: Create interaction features for geometrical ratios\nWe can create new features by combining related geometrical features to capture latent patterns such as aspect ratios of major components of vehicles.\n\n```python\n# INTERACTION_1: Product of PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO and MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO\n# Feature description: Combining primary axis aspect ratio with max length aspect ratio, reflecting vehicle shape proportionality in major and minor dimensions.\n# Usefulness: This can help discern vehicles with similar individual ratios but differing shapes, e.g., longer and narrower vehicles like vans.\n\ndf['INTERACTION_1'] = df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'] * df['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO']\n\n# INTERACTION_2: Ratio of SCATTER_RATIO to ELONGATEDNESS\n# Feature description: Scatter ratio over elongatedness indicates how evenly the shape is distributed along minor and major axes.\n# Usefulness: This could helped distinguish between shapes with scattered structures (e.g., buses) and elongated ones (e.g., sedans).\n\ndf['INTERACTION_2'] = df['SCATTER_RATIO'] / df['ELONGATEDNESS']\n\n# INTERACTION_3: Sum of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS and SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS\n# Feature description: Combining major and minor axis scaled variances to capture the overall spread of the vehicle silhouette.\n# Usefulness: Helps identify vehicles with similar overall dimensions but different internal variance distributions (e.g., compact vs. spread out vehicles).\n\ndf['INTERACTION_3'] = df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] + df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS']\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Apply transformations to normalize skewed continuous features\nSince some features (e.g., SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS, RADIUS_RATIO) appear heavily skewed or have broad ranges, applying transformations like log or z-score normalization can stabilize their distributions.\n\n```python\n# LOG_RADIUS_RATIO: Logarithmic transformation of RADIUS_RATIO\n# Feature description: Normalized version of RADIUS_RATIO using logarithmic scaling.\n# Usefulness: Reduces the impact of extreme variances, especially for values that deviate significantly from the mean.\n\nimport numpy as np\ndf['LOG_RADIUS_RATIO'] = np.log1p(df['RADIUS_RATIO'])\n\n# ZSCALED_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS: Z-score transformation of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS\n# Feature description: Normalized version of the variance along the minor axis.\n# Usefulness: Normalizing features improves downstream model performance, especially when they have large standard deviations or distributions with long tails.\n\ndf['ZSCALED_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] = (df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] - df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'].mean()) / df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'].std()\n\n# SQRT_HOLLOWS_RATIO: Square root transformation of HOLLOWS_RATIO\n# Feature description: Nonlinear transformation to reduce skewness in HOLLOWS_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: This can stabilize variance and improve usefulness for separating vehicle types where hollow areas vary subtly but systematically.\n\ndf['SQRT_HOLLOWS_RATIO'] = np.sqrt(df['HOLLOWS_RATIO'])\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Encode categorical ranges and feature discretization\nDiscretization or binning of numerical features can help tree-based models discern better thresholds, while encoding categorical patterns can also be significant.\n\n```python\n# BIN_PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO: Binned version of PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO\n# Feature description: Assigning bins to PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO to group vehicles with similar profile aspect ratios.\n# Usefulness: Captures non-linear relationships by denoting group characteristics instead of treating values numerically.\n\ndf['BIN_PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'] = pd.cut(df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO'], bins=5, labels=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\n# ONE_HOT_SK\n    \n", 'Here are the three possible next steps to generate additional features or improve the existing features:\n\n### Step 1: Feature Interaction - Combine key geometric properties\nCombine features that can reveal important interdependencies between attributes, based on the available physical and topological information in the dataset.\n\n```python\n# radius_aspect_ratio\n# This feature captures the ratio between the RADIUS_RATIO and PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: Helps encode shapes with pronounced radius variation relative to their axis aspect ratio. This might help differentiate vehicles with more eccentric shapes (e.g., SAAB vs. VAN).\ndf[\'radius_aspect_ratio\'] = df[\'RADIUS_RATIO\'] / df[\'PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO\']\n\n# circumference_to_area_ratio\n# This feature calculates the ratio of COMPACTNESS to CIRCULARITY, representing how efficiently the boundary encapsulates the area of the silhouette.\n# Usefulness: As vehicles vary in edge compactness versus area (e.g., VANs would have squarer shapes, buses more rectangular), this emphasizes shape properties.\ndf[\'circumference_to_area_ratio\'] = df[\'COMPACTNESS\'] / df[\'CIRCULARITY\']\n\n# variance_ratio\n# This feature creates a ratio of SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS to SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS.\n# Usefulness: Indicates how much the shape deviates along the major vs. minor axes, capturing elongatedness or skewness useful to distinguish between stretched or compact vehicles.\ndf[\'variance_ratio\'] = df[\'SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS\'] / df[\'SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS\']\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Apply Nonlinear Transformations - Handle skewness and amplify differences\nApply non-linear transformations to the highly skewed or wide-range features to simplify the distribution and enhance signal for models.\n\n```python\n# log_scaled_radius_of_gyration\n# Log-transform SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION to handle potential skewness and highlight relative changes better.\n# Usefulness: Reduces the impact of extreme values in a feature with a wide range, thereby making patterns more detectable for downstream models.\ndf[\'log_scaled_radius_of_gyration\'] = np.log1p(df[\'SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION\'])\n\n# sqrt_skewness_about_minor_axis\n# Applies a square root transformation to SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS to reduce skewness.\n# Usefulness: SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has a range skewed to small values; this focuses on relative differences across subtle shapes.\ndf[\'sqrt_skewness_about_minor_axis\'] = np.sqrt(df[\'SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\'])\n\n# reciprocal_kurtosis_about_major_axis\n# Apply a reciprocal transformation to KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS to emphasize small differences in higher values.\n# Usefulness: Highlights the outlier structure of higher kurtosis values, which could reflect distinct vehicle classes.\ndf[\'reciprocal_kurtosis_about_major_axis\'] = 1 / (df[\'KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS\'] + 1e-6)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Aggregations and Scaling - Normalize and bin features\nStandardize and contextualize features to make them more interpretable and robust to magnitude differences.\n\n```python\n# zscore_scatter_ratio\n# Standardize SCATTER_RATIO using z-score normalization.\n# Usefulness: Normalizes the differences in this feature to minimize the effects of large variances between individual samples.\ndf[\'zscore_scatter_ratio\'] = (df[\'SCATTER_RATIO\'] - df[\'SCATTER_RATIO\'].mean()) / df[\'SCATTER_RATIO\'].std()\n\n# binned_hollows_ratio\n# Bin the HOLLOWS_RATIO into discrete categories (low, medium, high) based on quartiles.\n# Usefulness: Creates categorical distinctions to capture the “hollowness” levels of a vehicle\'s 2D silhouette.\ndf[\'binned_hollows_ratio\'] = pd.qcut(df[\'HOLLOWS_RATIO\'], q=4, labels=[\'low\', \'medium-low\', \'medium-high\', \'high\'])\n\n# rolling_mean_distance_circularity\n# Apply a rolling mean with a window size of 3 to DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY.\n# Usefulness: Smoothens local variations which can be useful if the calculation of DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY is noisy.\ndf[\'rolling_mean_distance_circularity\'] = df[\'DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY\'].rolling(window=3, min_periods=1).mean()\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Optional Column Drops\nAfter feature engineering, consider dropping original columns that are now redundant, to simplify the model input.\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY is dropped\n# This feature might no longer provide additional value if "variance_ratio" already effectively captures differences in shape elongation.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY\'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# This feature is dropped after sqrt transformation as it is skewed and its effect is now captured by the new feature.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS\'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# This feature is converted into a binned version that may be more suitable for classification purposes.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'HOLLOWS_RATIO\'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Reasoning\n1. **Feature Interactions:** Combining key shape-related geometric properties (e.g., variance ratio, aspect ratios) can provide better information about vehicle class characteristics (e.g., elongatedness, compactness).\n2. **Nonlinear Transformations:** Using transformations mitigates extreme distributions, reduces skewness, and highlights relative differences between vehicle silhouettes.\n3. **Aggregations and Scaling:** Binning and normalization improve interpretability by standardizing scale differences while extracting broader patterns from raw numerical inputs.']
